  "name": "Son palenquero (San Basilio de Palenque sexteto)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Son palenquero is the sexteto music of San Basilio de Palenque, the maroon village near Cartagena, which reworked the Cuban son heard on mid-20th-century radio and records into a local form sung in Spanish and Palenquero creole. Its best-known group, the Sexteto Tabalá, plays marímbula (a large plucked lamellophone box bass), drums, maracas and claves under a lead singer and a small answering coro. The acoustic-bass card stands in for the marímbula and the hand-drum card for the sexteto's drum.",
  "recipe": "classic modern son palenquero, groove Percussion, Latin am branch. single leader group response, unprocessed natural acoustic, live take. natural acoustic bass credited acoustic bass, hand drum source unspecified, maracas, claves. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
